2. Consumer: Tail File Over SSH Tunnel with Ack Tracking (file_to_peer.sh)

To be 100% certain a SQL line was actually executed on the remote database (and not just sitting in an OS TCP buffer when the SSH tunnel snapped), have the remote mysql CLI print back an acknowledgment (SELECT <line_num>;) after each line, and only update <peer>.line once that ack comes back over the tunnel:
sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

# Arguments: <peer_name> <local_tunnel_port> <peer_mysql_cnf> <db_name>
PEER_NAME="$1"
TUNNEL_PORT="$2"
PEER_CNF="$3"
DB_NAME="$4"

SYNC_DIR="/var/lib/db_sync"
EVENTS_FILE="$SYNC_DIR/events.sql"
OFFSET_FILE="$SYNC_DIR/${PEER_NAME}.line"

# Start from line 1 if never synced before
if [ ! -f "$OFFSET_FILE" ]; then
    echo "1" > "$OFFSET_FILE"
fi

while true; do
    START_LINE=$(cat "$OFFSET_FILE")
    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Streaming $EVENTS_FILE from line $START_LINE to $PEER_NAME (port $TUNNEL_PORT)..."

    # 1. Tail events.sql from START_LINE, attach 'SELECT <line_num>;' after each SQL statement
    # 2. Pipe into persistent remote mysql CLI (--batch --skip-column-names)
    # 3. Only advance OFFSET_FILE when remote MySQL echoes back the confirmed line number
    tail -n +"$START_LINE" -f "$EVENTS_FILE" | \
    awk -v line="$START_LINE" '
        NF {
            print $0;
            print "SELECT " line ";";
            fflush();
            line++;
        }
    ' | \
    mysql --defaults-extra-file="$PEER_CNF" -h 127.0.0.1 -P "$TUNNEL_PORT" "$DB_NAME" \
        --batch --skip-column-names --unbuffered | \
    awk -v off_file="$OFFSET_FILE" '
        /^[0-9]+$/ {
            print ($1 + 1) > off_file;
            close(off_file);
        }
    ' || true

    echo "$(date -u +"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ") - Connection to $PEER_NAME interrupted. Resuming from $(cat "$OFFSET_FILE") in 3s..."
    sleep 3
done
Use code with caution.

Why This Is Such a Clean Solution

• Zero External Dependencies: Pure POSIX /bin/sh, tail, awk, ssh, and mysql—no Redis, no Kafka, no triggers, no outbox tables.
• True End-to-End Confirmation: Because SELECT <line>; runs after each INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on the remote MySQL server, OFFSET_FILE only increments after the remote database has actually committed the change. If the SSH tunnel breaks mid-flight, the unconfirmed line is automatically replayed upon reconnect (and safely handled by ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE).
• Easy Log Rotation: Once a week, once all three *.line files match wc -l < events.sql, you can truncate events.sql (: > events.sql) and reset the three *.line files to 1.
