That already works out of the box—mysqlbinlog --read-from-remote-server does not need SSH or filesystem access to the managed database.
It connects over the network to port 3306 using MySQL's standard replication protocol (just like a read replica does) and writes the local events.sql file onto your Linode VM's disk.
Here is the exact flow on each region:
1. Managed MySQL DB (Cloud): Sits behind its firewall, allowing connections only from your local Linode VM's IP over SSL.
2. Local Linode VM (binlog_to_file.sh): Connects to the local Managed MySQL DB over port 3306 using mysqlbinlog --read-from-remote-server, pulls the binlog stream across the private network, and writes /var/lib/db_sync/events.sql onto the VM's own local disk.
3. Local Linode VM (file_to_peer.sh): Reads /var/lib/db_sync/events.sql from the VM's local disk and streams confirmed lines over the SSH tunnel to the other regions' VMs (which then apply them to their own local Managed MySQL DBs).


