Only 2 Things to Check on Your Linode Managed MySQL Cluster

To make sure mysqlbinlog --read-from-remote-server can pull from your managed database remotely:
1. Replication Privilege: Make sure your user (akmadmin or a dedicated repl_user) has REPLICATION SLAVE and REPLICATION CLIENT privileges (GRANT REPLICATION SLAVE, REPLICATION CLIENT ON *.* TO 'repl_user'@'%';).
2. Binlog Retention: Make sure your Linode Managed MySQL cluster retains binary logs for at least a few hours (default on Aiven/Linode Managed MySQL is typically 24 hours with backups enabled) so if your VM reboots, mysqlbinlog can pick up right where binlog_node1.state left off.
