No—the only reason that awk script looked so complicated is because MySQL defaults to ROW binary logging, which outputs column numbers (@1, @2) instead of column names.
If you change one database parameter—or use a lightweight open-source binlog reader—the entire awk column-mapping script disappears.

Option 1: Set binlog_format = STATEMENT or MIXED (Simplest)

When MySQL uses STATEMENT (or MIXED for standard queries) instead of ROW, mysqlbinlog outputs literal, ready-to-execute SQL statements (INSERT INTO orders ...;) natively.
Your entire binlog_to_file.sh processor shrinks to 5 lines:
sh
mysqlbinlog \
    --defaults-extra-file="$SRC_CNF" \
    --read-from-remote-server \
    --stop-never \
    --database="$DB_NAME" \
    --start-position="$START_POS" \
    "$START_FILE" \

| grep -E '^(INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|REPLACE) ' >> "$EVENTS_FILE"
Use code with caution.
(Caveat: Check if Linode Managed MySQL's Advanced Configuration Parameters allow changing binlog_format from ROW to MIXED—Aiven/Linode locks ROW on some plans for replication safety).

Option 2: Use Maxwell's Daemon (Zero Custom Parsing, Works on ROW Format)

If your Linode Managed MySQL cluster locks binlog_format = ROW, don't write your own awk parser. Run Maxwell's Daemon—a single lightweight open-source tool made specifically to read MySQL's ROW binlog over SSL and output clean JSON with real column names automatically:
sh
maxwell \
  --host=your-cluster.servers.linodedb.net \
  --user=akmadmin \
  --password=your_password \
  --ssl=REQUIRED \
  --producer=file \
  --output_file=/var/lib/db_sync/events.json
Use code with caution.
Maxwell handles binlog positions, column names, multi-line text, and reconnects out of the box.

Option 3: Log SQL Directly in Your App Code (Simplest of All)

If you control your web application (e.g., PHP/Laravel, Node, Python), you can skip mysqlbinlog completely:
• Whenever your app executes a write query (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), have your database wrapper append that SQL statement (with origin_node_id = 'node1') straight to /var/lib/db_sync/events.sql.
• Your file_to_peer.sh SSH tunnel tailer then ships those exact lines to the other 3 regions immediately—with zero binlog parsing at all.
